    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d322">

                <pb facs="0391" n="379" sameAs="frus:frus1941v02/pg379"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/12055: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Algiers</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cole</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Algiers</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-06-14T19:00:00+01:00">June 14, 1941—7
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>244. From Murphy.<anchor n="23" xml:id="d322-f1"/> I learn from an excellent
                    source that a group of 20 German Armistice delegates arrived here yesterday to
                    take possession of military automotive equipment stored in Algeria since the
                    Armistice. This equipment, the exact amount of which I do not know, was
                    requisitioned shortly after the Armistice and delivery thereof is in accordance
                    with the terms of the Convention. However, my informant states that when the
                    equipment arrives in Libya it will prove worthless because great pains have been
                    taken to so doctor mechanical parts that even a slick used car dealer would be
                    impressed.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Vichy. [Murphy.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cole</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d322-f1" n="23" xml:id="d322-f1-content">
                <p>Robert D. Murphy, Counselor of Embassy in France, temporarily at Algiers, with
                    responsibility for supervision of the North African economic aid program.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
